<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Langkasuka</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 1474 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An early Malay port kingdom on the Kra Isthmus near modern Pattani, linking Indian Ocean and South China Sea trade for over a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chinese annals of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a> say the kingdom of Lang-ya-xiu was founded around the 2nd century, more than 400 years before their own time. It grew up on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, where traders crossed the narrow isthmus by land to avoid the long voyage round the peninsula. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarang">Yarang</a>, near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pattani-kingdom">Pattani</a>, have revealed brick religious buildings of the period.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chinese sources describe a walled city, a king who rode an elephant under a white canopy, and a court that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist practice. In 515 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagadatta">Bhagadatta</a> sent envoys to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> court. Its wealth came from transit trade and forest goods such as aloeswood and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>, and for long periods it fell within the orbit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thanjavur-maratha-kingdom">Tanjore</a> inscription of about 1030 lists Ilangasogam among the places his fleet attacked in 1025. The kingdom faded in later centuries, and its name survived in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah</a> chronicle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-merong-mahawangsa">Hikayat Merong Mahawangsa</a>. The Muslim <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pattani-kingdom">Pattani Kingdom</a>, which arose in the same area, is generally seen as its successor.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/langkasuka.jpg" alt="Langkasuka" width="320" height="264"><figcaption>Image: Southeast Asia blank political map.svg: Sylvanmoon (talk) / derivative work: Sisuvia · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Possible_extent_of_the_Langkasuka_kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 1474 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhagadatta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lang-ya-xiu, Lengkasuka, Lengjiaxiu, Langyaxiu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=787&amp;focus=langkasuka" aria-label="Langkasuka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7435 1606 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Langkasuka lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 2434h0M7187 2016h0M7798 2191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7755 1862h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7755 1862h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Thailand. <a href="/atlas?year=787&amp;focus=langkasuka">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
